  "brokeAndFixed": [
    {
      "title": "An unreachable web host stalled the whole outbox",
      "what": "In the first webhook-storm run, 59 outbox messages were still waiting a minute after the load, and Redis had not yet counted a single seat sold. The page-revalidation call (after an event is published) went to a web host that did not answer; its HTTP client retried within a 30 s budget, and the outbox handles messages one after another, so inventory commits, emails and refunds queued behind it.",
      "fix": "The revalidation call gets one 3 s attempt with no client-side retries; the outbox already retries a failed message with its own backoff. Pinned by an integration test against a host that accepts connections and never answers. The outbox lag gauge and its Grafana alert (> 30 s) now make this visible during a sale."
    },
    {
      "title": "Postgres ran out of connections at 100 buyers",
      "what": "The first sale run (spec 08, 2026-11-10) at 100 concurrent buyers answered 6 logins with 500 (53300 too many clients): two .NET connection pools of up to 100 each, against Postgres' default of 100. The invariants still held.",
      "fix": "The load-test stack runs Postgres with max_connections = 300. In production the same sizing rule applies: pool size × services must stay under max_connections."
    },
    {
      "title": "Latency budgets missed on the laptop",
      "what": "With 100 concurrent buyers in the buy flow, hold p99 was about 620 ms and checkout p99 about 920 ms against budgets of 150 and 300 ms; at the soak's steady 2 buyers per second they were 51 and 35 ms. The stack, Postgres, Redis, the observability stack and k6 share one laptop's CPU.",
      "fix": "Not fixed, and not hidden: the budgets are the spec's for a dedicated machine and are shown as missed. Correctness did not depend on it: every invariant held in every scenario."
    }
  ]
}
